hi there steve kaufman here again  um  i'm continuing in my series of  frequently asked questions about  language learning  uh some of you follow my blog the  linguist on language some of you are  members of link  um i'm i talk from my own experience not  from any great scientific research  it may surprise some of you that one of  the questions that i am asked most often  is  should i learn several languages at the  same time or should i just focus on one  at a time  now many of you have probably  you know not yet succeeded in learning  one foreign language to the level that  you would like so you may find it  surprising that people are asking me  about learning multiple languages  but in fact you know once you've  conquered your first language  you very quickly start looking at a  second and a third and a fourth  foreign language to learn  it's not that difficult to do once you  get the hang of it  so what's my take on this  uh you know all of these things depend  on your individual  you know preferences and so forth  i find it difficult to concentrate on  more than one language at a time  that is to say if i'm starting a new  language or i'm still  in the middle of that period where we're  trying to achieve a certain level in the  language i find it difficult to take  myself away from  my main love  to then focus on another  you know  predilection so to speak so  uh i'll typically go very hard at one  language and and i believe there's value  in intensity that the more intensively  or intensely you pursue a language the  better so if you have an hour a day to  spend on language learning or two or  three or half an hour whatever it might  be  i think there's an advantage in focusing  that  on that one language so the day after  day after day you're with that one  language i think you're going to achieve  more  i will sometimes sort of go 80 90 on one  language and then spend 10 20 on a minor  language which you know in the case say  of portuguese is so similar to spanish  that it's it's kind of less effort  but i find it difficult to take myself  away from my main  uh preoccupation my main love at a given  moment which for example for me for the  for the last three four years has been  russian  uh i may get away from that and move on  to something else  so it's entirely up to you i know there  are people who  simultaneously study three or four  languages  i would recommend that you at least  focus on one for a given period of time  whether it be three months six months  give it your best shot go at it with a  lot of intensity then leave it whatever  level you've achieved there and go on to  another one a few other bits of advice  if you leave a language after three or  six months you won't forget it if you  study another language when you go back  to that first language you will actually  find that you've improved i found this  over and over again yeah you'll be rusty  at first but  studying a second or third language is  all part of improving your ability to  learn languages and when you go back to  that  first language the second language the  first foreign language you will have  been you will have improved so there's  no danger in studying two or three or  four languages whether you do them at  the same time or whether you do them  sort of in sequence i prefer to focus on  one at a time so there you have it  that's my take on studying multiple  languages i hope that's helpful  i should add let me know what questions  you have and they can be general  questions about language learning or  they can be specific questions about how  do i deal with the subjunctive in  spanish or the you know case endings in  russian or any specific language problem  that you have learning the characters in  chinese whatever it might be let me know  what you are interested in so thank you  for listening
 

